  "fixture": "contracts/fixtures/statement_events.dbs",
  "note": "Statement events (docs/operations/usage.md \"Statement events\"). Each event holds the seven fields of INT-91. Each case runs on a new case database with the fixture installed (unless install is false), opens a new connection, registers one recording subscriber, runs the steps in order and compares the recorded events of every dialect: sql, binds, kind, tables, transaction (renumbered from 1 in the order of appearance within the case, null outside a transaction) and error (the error code of the statement, or null). With compare \"tables\" only the events that name a table are compared. elapsed is checked to be non-negative. Step ops: create (insert a row with label), get (read the row with label), count (count the rows; result is the expected count), update (read the row with label, then change its label to `to`), delete (read the row with label, then delete it), transaction (run steps in a transaction; fail makes the callback fail after its steps with an error of its own, reported as error \"callback\"), install (install the fixture), fail_subscriber (register a second subscriber that fails on the first event of kind), stop_failing (remove it), unsubscribe (remove the recording subscriber). A step with error expects the operation to fail with that code; \"callback\" is the error of the callback itself.",
  "server_transactions": [
    {
      "database": "mysql",
      "first_run_clients": [
        "go",
        "php",
        "php-extension",
        "python",
        "rust",
        "typescript"
      ],
      "first_run_prepare_clients": [],
      "note": "MySQL. probe sums the transaction count of the connection's thread in performance_schema, which grows by one for every statement that opens a transactional table; a read of performance_schema opens none, so the probe costs no transaction (probe_cost 0). The summary is kept only while the transaction instrument, the global, thread and current-transaction consumers and the instrumentation of the thread are on; setup counts them and must return setup_want, so a server whose configuration turns one off fails instead of counting 0. Measured on 2026-10-31 on MySQL 8.4.11 with make run-databases TARGETS=parity-check UNITS=statement-events-server-transactions, first / second run transactions for 8 events of 5 statement texts: go 8 / 8, php 8 / 8, php-extension 8 / 8, python 8 / 8, rust 8 / 8, typescript 8 / 8. A MySQL prepare (COM_STMT_PREPARE) opens no transaction, so every client is in first_run_clients.",
      "page": "model_statements",
      "probe": "SELECT CAST(COALESCE(SUM(COUNT_STAR), 0) AS SIGNED) FROM performance_schema.events_transactions_summary_by_thread_by_event_name WHERE THREAD_ID = PS_CURRENT_THREAD_ID() AND EVENT_NAME = 'transaction'",
      "probe_cost": 0,
      "setup": "SELECT CAST((SELECT COUNT(*) FROM performance_schema.setup_instruments WHERE NAME = 'transaction' AND ENABLED = 'YES') + (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM performance_schema.setup_consumers WHERE NAME IN ('global_instrumentation', 'thread_instrumentation', 'events_transactions_current') AND ENABLED = 'YES') + (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM performance_schema.threads WHERE THREAD_ID = PS_CURRENT_THREAD_ID() AND INSTRUMENTED = 'YES') AS SIGNED)",
      "setup_want": 5
    },
    {
      "database": "postgres",
      "first_run_clients": [
        "php",
        "php-extension",
        "typescript"
      ],
      "first_run_prepare_clients": [
        "go",
        "python",
        "rust"
      ],
      "note": "PostgreSQL. probe returns the local transaction number of the backend, which grows by one for every server transaction, including the implicit transaction of a Parse that a driver sends with its own Sync, and costs one transaction itself (probe_cost). The connection's TimeZone must be UTC from a startup parameter (pg_settings source client), not from a SET statement. Through a pooler (ORM_TEST_POSTGRES_DSN differs from ORM_TEST_POSTGRES_SERVER_DSN) the pooler applies the client's startup parameters to its server connection with SET, so the source says nothing about the client there and only the setting is compared; the transaction counts are the same on both paths. A client whose connection lives for one request (PHP) sends each statement in one round trip; a pooled client prepares a statement text once per connection and then reuses it (docs/operations/perf.md, F1). TypeScript's `pg` driver sends the Parse of a named statement together with its first Bind and Execute. Go (the pgx statement cache), Rust (sqlx) and Python (psycopg with prepare_threshold 0) prepare a new statement text in a round trip of its own before its first execution on a connection; Go and Rust declare the parameter types the plan gives without reading the catalog. Measured on 2026-10-31 on PostgreSQL 17.11 with the same command, first / second run: go 13 / 8, rust 13 / 8, php 8 / 8, php-extension 8 / 8, typescript 8 / 8; python 13 / 8 with prepare_threshold 0 (T329), where psycopg 3.3.6 at its default threshold of 5 measured 8 / 9, a Parse with its own Sync on the fifth execution of a text.",
      "page": "model_statements",
      "probe": "SELECT split_part(virtualtransaction, '/', 2)::bigint FROM pg_locks WHERE pid = pg_backend_pid() AND locktype = 'virtualxid'",
      "probe_cost": 1,
      "time_zone": {
        "setting": "UTC",
        "source": "client"
      }
    }
  ],
